description:
Representing URIs as `string`s can prove to be a security risk as they are difficult to parse, validate and encode. It is therefore recommended that you use the more safer and reliable built-in alternative `System.URI`.

<!--more-->
### Bad Practice
```cs
public string getEndpointUri()
{
    return _endpoint;
}
```

### Recommended
```cs
public Uri getEndpointUri()
{
    return new URI(_endpoint);
}
```

## Reference
- [Uri Class](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.uri?view=net-6.0)

A variable is declared but not used within its scope.
Unused variables can clutter the codebase, reduce readability, and potentially indicate logical errors or incomplete implementations.
If you intend to keep a variable unused intentionally, you can use an underscore (_) as the variable name.

<!--more-->

### Bad Practice
```csharp
void UnusedVariable()
{
    int x = 10;
}
```

### Recommended
```csharp
void UsedVariable()
{
    int x = 10;
    Console.WriteLine(x);
}
```

```csharp
void IntentionallyUnusedVariable()
{
    int _ = 10;
    // intentionally left unused
}
```

