Prefer using explicit casts by calling `Number`, `Boolean`, or `String` over using operators like `+`, `!!` or `"" +`.
This is considered best practice as it improves readability.

### Bad Practice
```js
const b = !!foo;
// The `+` operator does not change the value of its operand
// unless it's already a number.
let n = +foo;
n = 1 * foo;
const s = "" + foo;
```

### Recommended

```js
const b = Boolean(foo);
const n = Number(foo);
const s = String(foo);
```

The `?.` operator allows you to access a property or a member only if it is non-null. Consider simplifying the highlighted expression, i.e. the object and its member's null checks via the usage of this `?.` operator.

<!--more-->
### Bad Practice
```csharp
if (foo == null || foo.bar == null)
{
    // ...
}
```

### Recommended
```csharp
if (foo?.bar == null)
{
    // ...
}
```

